The current trame-slicer Medical Viewer application is not well adapted to
mobile users. It requires users to upload DICOM files, and it provides a large
number of tools that are difficult to use on a phone and do not align with
typical mobile workflows, which focus on viewing and commenting rather than
performing segmentation.

To showcase the capabilities of medical software in a commercial demonstration
while also driving the development of phone-oriented trame tools, a proof of
concept for a trame-slicer mobile web application was developed. A member of the
team acted as product owner and defined the needs of a target user persona,
along with the initial features required for the proof of concept:

\begin{itemize}

\item Load and review patient volumes

\item Place markups and annotations on specific slices

\item Write reports related to patient health and disease tracking

\end{itemize}

The application is developed in the \refrepo{Mobile-DICOM-viewer} repository, a
mobile web application for DICOM visualization based on
\refrepo{trame-slicer}.

Mobile interactions differ fundamentally from desktop ones: there is no
right-click, no keyboard, and the screen is small. During the development of the
application it became clear that the frameworks it relies on,
\refrepo{trame-rca}, \citev{vtk-js} and VTK \cite{VTK}, did not fully support
the gestures a phone requires. This work therefore led to a number of
contributions to these frameworks, described below.

\subsubsection{Gesture support in \refrepo{trame-rca}}

Interactions are forwarded between the vtk-js client and the server-side VTK
rendering by \refrepo{trame-rca}. On a phone, interactions are gestures, and the
event representations of vtk-js and of the VTK side did not match: two-finger
gestures such as pinch, rotate or pan were therefore not handled. Gesture
support was implemented in trame-rca by translating between both
representations in JavaScript as early as possible
(\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-rca/42} and \cref{pr:Kitware/trame-rca/44}).

Once the gestures worked, testing with a tablet revealed that they generated so
many events that they stacked up and took several seconds to reach the server.
The event throttling was mouse-specific and could not compress consecutive
gesture events; it was extended so that two consecutive gesture events of the
same type are now compressed together (\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-rca/48} and
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-rca/49}).

Mobile browsers also introduced specific issues. On iOS, the long press on an
image or canvas makes Safari select the element to share or download it, which
prevented LongTap detection; this was fixed with CSS properties so that the
Remote Controlled Area catches all interactions
(\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-rca/50}). Finally, as vtk-js began to emit Tap and
LongTap events, trame-rca was extended to forward them
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-rca/52}, closing the client side of the gesture chain
(\cref{fig:gesture-events}).

