gesture detection}{fig:gesture-detection}

\subsubsection{VTK}

Testing these gestures also exposed several bugs on the C++ side of VTK. The
\texttt{EndRotate} event was mapped to the wrong command, the pinch event
position was computed in normalized coordinates while local coordinates are
expected, which placed the zoom fix-point at the wrong location, and the pinch
scale was not correctly forwarded. These problems were fixed for the 9.7.0
release of VTK (\cref{fig:git-tree-vtk-gestures},
\cref{pr:vtk/vtk/13428}).

The pan gesture translation was also made cumulative, consistently with rotate
and pinch, which both give values relative to the beginning of the gesture, thus
reducing precision errors (\cref{pr:vtk/vtk/13570}).

Finally, Tap and LongTap events needed to be handled in VTK as well, to complete
the chain started by \cref{pr:Kitware/vtk-js/3596} and
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-rca/52} (\cref{pr:vtk/vtk/13618}). Until this change is
available in a VTK version Slicer is built with, the mobile application depends
on a patched VTK module and a custom \refrepo{trame-rca} branch.

\insertfigure{assets/images/git-tree-vtk-gestures}{width=\textwidth}{Git
tree of the VTK branch fixing the gesture events handling, from 9.6.2 to
9.7.0}{fig:git-tree-vtk-gestures}

Compiling and testing those versions of VTK were a real challenge, the build
process was very long. Trying out new gestures used in Slicer required to
compile VTK and Slicer after updating Slicer's code to use VTK's new API.

\subsubsection{Bugs fixed in Slicer}

The application runs with a single full-screen view, which exposed a
segmentation fault in Slicer's slice intersection computation when the layout
contains a single view. This overflow is fixed by returning early in that case
(\cref{pr:Slicer/Slicer/9334}). A build fix for
\definerepo{https://github.com/KitwareMedical/SlicerCore}{SlicerCore} was also
contributed (\cref{pr:KitwareMedical/SlicerCore/12}).

